INSERT INTO lesson_units (id, unit_no, title, source_path, summary, grammar_focus, level, raw_content) VALUES (88, 88, 'hardly any  ➜ Unit 101Csome  and  any', 'lecture/unit_088_hardly_any__➜_Unit_101Csome__and__any', 'hardly any  ➜ Unit 101Csome  and  any In general we use some (also somebody /someone /something ) in positive sentences and any (also anybody etc.) in negative sentences:', 'future', 'B1', '# บทเรียน: hardly any  ➜ Unit 101Csome  and  any

## เนื้อหาบทเรียนฉบับเต็ม (Detailed Content)

hardly any  ➜ Unit 101Csome  and  any
In general we use some (also somebody /someone /something ) in positive sentences and any 
(also anybody etc.) in negative sentences:
We use any in the following sentences because the meaning is negative:
She went out without any money. (she didn’t take any money with her)
He refused to eat anything . (he didn’t eat anything )
It’s a very easy exam.   Hardly anybody fails. (= almost nobody fails)
any bigger / no better etc. ➜ Unit 106BA
